.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt DISK 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm disk
.Nd disks, partitions, device nodes and identities
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Rach2 uses 512-byte sectors.
The disk probe tries AHCI before ATA; USB sticks also register as disks.
The registry has eight records, numbered in registration order.
A new disk reuses the lowest released number.
.Ss Device nodes
.Bl -tag -width /dev/diskNpM
.It Pa /dev/diskN
The whole disk numbered
.Ar N .
The node is owned by root, with mode 0400.
Whole-disk nodes are published for every disk by a recovery kernel or with
.Ql rach2.diskdiag
on the boot line.
A USB stick's whole-disk node is always published.
.It Pa /dev/diskNpM
Partition
.Ar M
of disk
.Ar N ,
with mode 0400.
MBR primary partitions use their table positions, 1 through 4.
Logical partitions are numbered from 5 in extended-chain order;
the extended entry itself has no node.
GPT partitions use their array positions, counted from 1.
.It Pa /dev/bootpart
MBR entry 1 when it is a FAT32 partition starting at or after LBA 2048,
smaller than 2 GiB and overlapping no other partition.
The node has mode 0600.
.It Pa /dev/grubcore
The range from LBA 1 through 2047, published when no partition overlaps it.
The node has mode 0600.
.El
.Pp
The stat interface reports whole-disk and partition nodes as block devices.
It reports
.Pa /dev/bootpart
and
.Pa /dev/grubcore
as character devices.
.Ss Partition tables
The partition service reads MBR tables with one extended chain, or GPT.
For GPT it tries the primary header and then the backup if necessary.
Out-of-range and overlapping entries are refused and counted.
At most fifteen partitions are accepted per disk.
.Ss Partition identities
The boot-line settings
.Ql rach2.root=SPEC
and
.Ql rach2.boot=SPEC
select partitions by identity.
The forms of
.Ar SPEC
are:
.Bl -tag -width MBRID=XXXXXXXX,M
.It Ql PARTUUID=GUID
A GPT partition GUID.
.It Ql MBRID=XXXXXXXX,M
An eight-digit hexadecimal MBR disk identifier and partition number.
.It Ql UUID=UUID
An ext2 volume UUID.
.It Ql LABEL=NAME
A GPT partition name or ext2 volume label.
.It Pa /dev/diskNpM
A partition node.
.El
.Pp
Exactly one partition must match.
A root identity that matches none or more than one mounts no root and
produces a diagnostic.
A boot identity must resolve to the first primary entry of an MBR disk
that passes the boot-window checks; otherwise neither boot device is
published.
.Xr growroot 8
uses the
.Ql MBRID=XXXXXXXX,M
form to identify its partition.
.Ss Reads and writes
Whole-disk and partition nodes accept reads of any byte range.
Raw writes and boot-window reads require sector-aligned offsets and lengths.
At most eight transfers have separate buffers at once.
.Pp
Only one write-capable descriptor may be open per disk across its nodes.
A second write-capable open returns
.Er EBUSY .
A mount claims its sector range.
A write-capable open over a claimed range returns
.Er EBUSY ,
as does a mount over an open writer's range.
.Pp
The ordinary kernel returns
.Er EPERM
for writes through whole-disk and partition nodes.
The recovery kernel permits root to write them.
The metal kernel's ATA and AHCI driver policy permits only reads.
A raw write to a write-protected USB stick returns
.Er EIO .
.Pp
.Dv BLKRRPART
on a whole-disk node rereads the partition table.
It returns
.Er EBUSY
while a partition is mounted or another descriptor is open on the disk.
.Ss Disk information
.Pa /proc/disk
reports the probe state, driver policy and counters for commands, flushes,
polls, wait ticks, requests in flight, overlaps, quarantines and releases.
The detailed record describes disk 0, including its model, serial and
firmware, with
.Ql part1
through
.Ql part4
lines.
It is followed by a line per disk in the form
.Dl disk N TRANSPORT LOCATION SECTORS TABLE MODEL
with an
.Ql identity N ...
line for its serial and a
.Ql part N ...
line for each partition.
.Ss Sealed disk
The sealed disk has three partitions:
.Bl -enum
.It
The FAT32 boot partition: GRUB, the kernel slots, the menu and the
environment block.
.It
The recovery system's image.
.It
The ext2 root.
.El
.Pp
The GRUB core image occupies the gap before partition 1.
.Ss Loop devices
.Pa /dev/loop0
through
.Pa /dev/loop3
provide block devices over regular files.
See
.Xr loopctl 8 .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/diskNpM
.It Pa /dev/diskN
Whole-disk nodes.
.It Pa /dev/diskNpM
Partition nodes.
.It Pa /dev/bootpart
Boot-partition window.
.It Pa /dev/grubcore
GRUB core-image window.
.It Pa /dev/loop0 , /dev/loop1 , /dev/loop2 , /dev/loop3
Devices over regular files.
.It Pa /proc/disk
Disk records and counters.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr recovery 7 ,
.Xr bootctl 8 ,
.Xr growroot 8 ,
.Xr loopctl 8 ,
.Xr mount 8 ,
.Xr rach2-install 8 ,
.Xr reflash 8
